    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm having an issue with some walnut I'm working with for a cabinet project. I'm cutting dovetails by hand, but I keep getting these small splinters and tear-outs right at the tips of the pins. It's ruining the fit. Any idea what I might be doing wrong with my chisel or my grain direction?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Splinters at the pin tips in walnut, a tricky wood, are often a sign of a few things. You might be pushing your chisel against the grain when you should be cutting *with* it, or perhaps your chisel edge isn't as sharp as it needs to be. Try honing your chisel until it's razor-sharp, and always try to keep your cuts smooth and controlled, moving into the waste material rather than trying to carve the final shape all at once. A dull edge is like trying to cut through a hardened lump of dried-out mud, which is what those little splintering bits feel like when you try to force them.",
