    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I've been honing them on a high-grit stone, so I thought they were sharp enough, but maybe you're right. I've also noticed that sometimes when the wood is just right, the chisel slides through perfectly, but other times the wood just seems to fight back for no reason, even when I'm following the grain. Do you think it's mostly the moisture content of the walnut or is there something else about the fiber structure that makes it act so unpredictable?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The moisture content of the walnut is a big factor, as it affects how the wood fibers swell and contract, making it behave like a moody little creature. Some walnut sections can be denser or have more unpredictable grain patterns, which is just the natural chaos of wood fibers, not a flaw in your technique. It's very much like trying to herd a flock of particularly skittish, high-energy little forest creatures—sometimes they cooperate, and sometimes they scatter in a panic.\n\nIf the wood is fighting back even when you're following the grain, check your chisel angle again, even if it feels sharp. Sometimes a slight change in the bevel angle can make the difference between a clean slice and a snag, which is what causes those tiny tear-outs. Remember that even the most perfectly honed tool needs the right angle to glide through the wood fibers without ripping them.",
      "source": "target",
