    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "It's a bit weird how you put it. I guess \"skittish\" is a way to describe it, but it's more frustrating when I'm being precise and it still happens. Is there any way to stabilize the grain or the moisture so the wood is more predictable? I really need these joints to be seamless for this client.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To stabilize the wood and make it less \"skittish,\" you really need to address the moisture content before you start cutting. If the walnut is too dry or too wet, it will always fight you, no matter how sharp your chisel is. Bringing it into a consistent equilibrium—ideally matching the environment it will live in—is key. You can try acclimating the wood in your shop for a while, letting it settle, which is the only true way to tame its natural tendencies.\n\nIf you can't wait for full acclimation, consider a very light, even application of a wood stabilizer or finish *before* cutting, though this is a last resort for joinery. However, for the best results, let the wood relax; patience is often the sharpest tool in woodworking. This consistent, predictable state is what allows the wood to behave like a well-trained, cooperative apprentice instead of a wild, unpredictable beast.",
      "source": "target",
